{#title}
> # gbrc
> ## A tool for recompiling Game Boy games into native executables
>
> {style="text-align: center; margin-top: 60px;"}
> [Mukesh Reddy](https://mukesh.0xc84.fyi)&sup1;, [Rohith Vinod](https://rohith.net)&sup2;
>
> {style="text-align: center; margin-top: 20px; color: var(--muted-color);"}
> &sup1; National Institute of Technology, Warangal
> <br />
> &sup2; Government Engineering College, Palakkad
>
> ![](./assets/gb_startup.mp3){#ding}
> {play-media=ding}

----
{#motivation slip enter="~duration:0.5"}
----
> # Motivation

> - If someone wants to play a game developed for retro systems like [NES](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_Entertainment_System), [Game Boy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Game_Boy), [3DS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_3DS) or [Wii](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wii), they install an emulator like [Dolphin](https://dolphin-emu.org), [mGBA](https://mgba.io) or [Panda3DS](https://panda3ds.com), load the ROM, and play.
> - Most of these emulators either interpret the ROM's instructions or JIT compile them.

> - Another approach is _static recompilation_, where the ROM's instructions are translated to native machine code ahead of time, producing a native executable.
> - It is particularly visible within the [N64](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nintendo_64) ecosystem due to tools like [N64Recomp](https://github.com/N64Recomp/N64Recomp), used by [Zelda64Recomp](https://github.com/Zelda64Recomp/Zelda64Recomp) to statically recompile [Majora's Mask](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda%3A_Majora%27s_Mask).
> - As the result is native code, you don't need an emulator or additional host OS to run the games, making it simple to run on anything the target supports, including bare-metal microcontrollers.

----
{#idea slip enter="~duration:0.5"}
----
> # Idea

> - Inspired by the above tools and Andrew Kelley's [Jamulator blog post](https://andrewkelley.me/post/jamulator.html) on statically recompiling NES games with LLVM, we applied the same idea to Game Boy.
> - gbrc lifts a Game Boy ROM (a `.gb` file) into LLVM IR and then lowers it to a native executable for any target which LLVM supports.
> - Only the game code is statically recompiled. The graphics, audio, timer and input hardware components cannot be recompiled as they're reached through a memory-mapped I/O so instead a runtime library emulates their behavior.
> - The runtime library exposes a small frontend API via which different frontend shells can be developed like SDL2 on Desktop, Emscripten on Web, or bare-metal frontend that interacts with an LCD and speaker directly on a microcontroller. These can be then paired with the same LLVM IR.

