{#technical-details-analyzer slip enter="~duration:0.5"}
----
> # Technical Details (Analyzer)

> - The CFG recovery is done using a worklist-style static discovery pass.
> - It starts from a set of known entry points and then recursively decodes opcodes until it hits a block terminator and records that span of opcodes as a single basic block.
> - As it decodes, it derives each instruction's possible successors and adds them to the worklist.
> - This handles instructions with static targets, but for instructions with dynamic targets like `JP HL`, `RET` and `RETI`, the successors can't be computed at compile time, so these are instead handled by a fallback emulator which is present in the runtime library.

----
{#technical-details-codegen slip enter="~duration:0.5"}
----
> # Technical Details (Codegen)

{#technical-details-codegen-part-1}
> - All the registers, status flags, and additional machine state information are stored as global variables.
>
> {.figure-row}
> ![](./assets/codegen_global_variables.png)

{pause=technical-details-codegen-part-1 up="~duration:0.5 technical-details-codegen-part-1"}

{#technical-details-codegen-part-2}
> - A special global variable called `g_budget` is used to know when to hand off the execution to render the graphics and audio i.e. tick PPU, APU and timer. It is set to the current machine cycle count + one frame's worth of machine cycles (17556) after each frame.

{pause=technical-details-codegen-part-2 up="~duration:0.5 technical-details-codegen-part-2"}

{#technical-details-codegen-part-3}
> - The program's entrypoint in LLVM IR is `rom_main` function which contains one label per basic block and each distinct instruction is emitted as a helper function.
> - After a block finishes, it jumps to the return dispatcher, the central hub that decides where execution goes next. On each visit it performs the following checks, in order:
>   - Is cycle deadline exhausted i.e. `cycles` >= `g_budget`? If yes, it returns, letting the runtime take over and tick PPU, timer and APU for that frame.
>   - Checks whether interrupts are enabled using `IME` flag and is an interrupt is pending. If so, it services it and updates the program counter to that particular vector.
>   - The LLVM IR maintains a lookup table of program counter to basic block. If the lookup fails, then it means that IR lifting process failed to capture that and it falls back to the emulator.

{pause=technical-details-codegen-part-3 up="~duration:0.5 technical-details-codegen-part-3"}

{pause focus="~duration:0.5 technical-details-codegen"}

----
{#future-plans slip enter="~duration:0.5"}
----
> # Future Plans

> - Adding support for memory bank controllers (MBCs) to be able to run cartridges larger than 32 KiB.
> - Adding support for Game Boy Color (GBC), which is pretty much like DMG-01 but with additional features.
> - Improving the static discovery pass with jump-table detection and value analysis to resolve dynamic targets up till a good coverage without an additional profile run.

